      "stageRecord": "The inflected verb raises from its established V position and adjoins to I, leaving a silent head trace. This analysis adopts overt V-to-I movement for the Swahili finite verb; movement positions the already inflected lexical word and does not manufacture its morphological spelling. The resulting complex I head contains the pronounced verb and an unpronounced inflectional feature head. The lower V trace remains connected to the moved lexical governor, preserving the government dependency of the passive object trace. A silent declarative C projects CP over the completed IP. The pronounced yield is exactly Vitabu hivi vimesomwa na watoto, with watoto interpreted as the readers.",
